.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
full 3D specialisation of the \fBTensorVariable\fP class template 


.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
S\&.K\&. Matthai 

.PP
Stephen G\&. Roberts 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
2001
.RE
.PP
.SH "Motivation"
.PP
Template specialization implementing optimized tensors in CSP\&. Depending on the spatial dimension of the model, tensor variables are either 2 x 2 or 3 x 3 matrices\&. Each value is associated with a flag which can be set to indicate to finite-element computations that the corresponding degree of freedom is enabled or restricted\&.
.SH "Functionality"
.PP
Apart from the standard constructors with a default where all elements are initialized to NaN (not-a-number) and the flags are set to plain, assignment is defined to allow the storage of tensors in STL containers\&. Ordering using STL's less() is facilitated through an \fBoperator<()\fP that compares the determinants of matrices\&. All costly operations like this are unrolled\&.

.PP
\fBTensorVariable\fP implements typical accessors, mutators for tensor elements and operations on entire tensors\&. To get at element 1,2 of 3D tensor, ts, use ts(1,2), for instance\&.

.PP
Value-by-value operations such as matrix addition, subtraction, division are implemented by the operators +, -, /\&. operator*() assumes different meanings dependent on the type of its arguments\&. Thus, tensors can be multiplied with scalars, vectors, and tensors to produce the standard results expected for matrix algebra\&. Other matrix algebraic operations include finding the \fBTransposed()\fP, \fBDeterminant()\fP, \fBInverse()\fP, \fBIdentity()\fP, and \fBAdjoint()\fP matrices\&.

.PP
The *= operator will always produce a temporary matrix\&. Therefore, should be preferred if the result is to be assigned to a new variable\&.

.PP
Assignent of a vector to a tensor is done by placing the vector in the tensors diagonal and zeroing its off-diagonal elements\&.

.PP
Matrices can be input and output via the console, using the \fBIn()\fP and \fBOut()\fP member functions\&.
.SH "Motivation"
.PP
Part of a consistent framework for physical variables for which there exist overloaded operators so that vector-matrix multiplications and other operations can be written in a straight forward way\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Using the template specialization mechanism of C++\&. Specializations exist only for the 2 and 3D cases\&. These are declared in this (3D) and the header file '\fBTensorVariable2\&.h\fP' (2D), respectively\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To diagonalize a tensor variable, one could write:

.PP
.PP
.nf
VectorVariable<double,2> vc(PLAIN,PLAIN,1,1);
TensorVariable<double,2> ts;

ts\&.Identity();
vc = ts * vc;

vc\&.Out();
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
(2-D) Const/Non const access operator convention not consistent with VectorVariable/ArrayVariable 
.RE
.PP

