.SS "bool \fBcsmp::TensorVariable\fP< 3U >::Eigen (\fBVectorVariable\fP< 3U > & evals, \fBTensorVariable\fP< 3U > & evecs, bool bNormalize) const"

.PP
assuming that the tensor is symmetric and positive definite, method returns Eigenvalues and vectors that can be normalised to 1 Computes eigenvalues and eigenvectors assuming that the tensor variable is symmetric\&. If not symmetric, off-diagonal elements are averaged\&. No check of symmetry is performed\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
the Eigenvectors are returned row by row into the evecs tensor\&.
.RE
.PP
By default the length of the eigenvectors is equivalent to the eigenvalues but it can be normalized to one\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Flag to normalize length of eigenvectors to 1\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Eigenvalues and vectors are returned into the supplied Vector and TensorVariables, respectively\&. Method returns false if the rank of the matrix is zero\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Cubic root finding algorithm using Cardano's formula and the eigenvectors are calculated via LU-backsubstitution\&.

.PP
\fBTest\fP
.RS 4
SKM - observed, by comparison with Maple, that all the values are the same, but that the first element of the second Eigenvector always has the opposite sign (does this matter?)
.RE
.PP

.SS "bool \fBcsmp::TensorVariable\fP< 3U >::EigenNonSymmetric (\fBVectorVariable\fP< 3U > & eigenVals, \fBTensorVariable\fP< 3U > & eigenVecs) const"

.PP
alternative Eigen decomposition that should also work for non-symmetric matrices Computes eigenvalues and eigenvectors for 3d \fBTensorVariable\fP (doesn't have to be symmetric)
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
eigenVectors, eigenValues

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Eigenvalues and vectors are returned into the supplied Vector and TensorVariables, respectively\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Uses: 1) Symmetric Householder reduction to tridiagonal form, derived from the Algol procedures tred2 by Bowdler, Martin, Reinsch, and Wilkinson, Handbook for Auto\&. Comp\&., Vol\&.ii-Linear Algebra, and the corresponding Fortran subroutine in EISPACK\&. 2) Symmetric tridiagonal QL algorithm, derived from the Algol procedures tql2, by Bowdler, Martin, Reinsch, and Wilkinson, Handbook for Auto\&. Comp\&., Vol\&.ii-Linear Algebra, and the corresponding Fortran subroutine in EISPACK\&.

.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
Lukas Mosser? 
.RE
.PP

.SS "bool \fBcsmp::TensorVariable\fP< 3U >::EigenValuesPositiveDefiniteSymmetricMatrix (double & eigenValue0, double & eigenValue1, double & eigenValue2) const"

.PP
assuming that the tensor is symmetric and positive definite, method returns its sorted Eigen values (largest to smallest) Code revision by Hossein Ageshlui (Nov\&. 2015), fixing several errors:

.PP
Previous code included two functions for eigenvalue calculation, using basically the same code, one outputing eigenvalues as a vector and the other one as an object of the class \fBVectorVariable<3U>\fP&\&.

.PP
To shorten the code and to not to cause any issues with previously written code, this new function has been added which calculates eigenvalues as scalar variables\&.

.PP
The old functions now only call this new function and output the eigenvalues as a vector or as an object of a \fBVectorVariable\fP\&. The new code also sorts the eigenvalues in descending order\&.

.PP
Also all the pow (1\&./3\&.) calls are replaced with cbrt()\&. The pow (1\&./\&.3) does not produce a result when used with negative values and it is slow\&. The source for solving the cubic equation is: http://mathworld.wolfram.com/CubicFormula.html 
